    "title": "A Menger-type min-max for monochromatic-cycle destruction",
    "description": "In an edge-colored graph, define an obstruction to be a monochromatic cycle (a cycle all of whose edges share one color). Conjecture: the minimum number of edges whose deletion makes every color class a forest equals the maximum number of edge-disjoint monochromatic cycles. Approach: exploit the fact that minimal obstructions are single monochromatic cycles, turning the 'cure' problem into a covering problem dual to a packing of monochromatic cycles, and seek an exact min-max identity by adapting augmenting-structure and LP-duality techniques from graph connectivity.",
    "tags": ["Combinatorics", "GraphColoring", "MinMax"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Palette thresholds for unavoidable monochromatic cycles",
    "description": "Since 'no monochromatic cycle' is equivalent to partitioning the edges into that many forests, the number of colors needed to avoid a monochromatic cycle is exactly the arboricity. Conjecture: for every graph of average degree at least d, any coloring with fewer than f(d) colors contains a monochromatic cycle, with f(d) growing linearly in d. Approach: relate the color threshold to Nash-Williams arboricity bounds and determine the sharp linear constant, including extremal near-regular constructions.",
    "tags": ["Combinatorics", "Arboricity", "ExtremalGraphTheory"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Stability of minimal obstructions to rainbow forests",
    "description": "The exact result states that a minimal obstruction (a colored graph with a monochromatic cycle, edge-minimal for that property) is a single monochromatic cycle plus isolated vertices. Conjecture (stability): if a colored graph has a monochromatic cycle but only t edges lie off every monochromatic cycle, then it is within t edge edits of a single monochromatic cycle plus isolated vertices. Approach: prove an approximate/robust counterpart of the exact classification, quantifying how sharply the single-cycle shape is enforced.",
    "tags": ["Combinatorics", "Stability", "ExtremalGraphTheory"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Directed rainbow branchings and monochromatic circuits",
    "description": "In an arc-colored directed graph, call a spanning out-forest in which each color is used acyclically a rainbow branching. Conjecture: the minimal digraphs admitting no rainbow branching are exactly the single monochromatic directed circuits together with isolated vertices. Approach: transfer the undirected proof, whose only geometric input is that a cycle not containing an edge survives that edge's deletion, to directed circuits, and handle the extra subtleties of out-forest (branching) structure.",
    "tags": ["Combinatorics", "DirectedGraphs", "RainbowStructures"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Matroidal generalization via colored circuits",
    "description": "The graph proof uses only that a circuit not containing a given element persists after deleting that element - a property of matroid circuits in general. Conjecture: for any matroid with colored ground set, the minimal subsets on which some color class is dependent are exactly the monochromatic circuits, with the graphic matroid case recovering single monochromatic cycles. Approach: reprove the cycle-persistence step abstractly for matroid circuits and connect the resulting theory to the rainbow-basis (Rado/Oxley) literature.",
    "tags": ["MatroidTheory", "Combinatorics", "RainbowStructures"]
